  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n- SPC Slight Risk for severe weather over much of the area today\n\nThe pattern continues to support the threat of thunderstorms over\nthe next 24 hours, including both today and tonight. At the\nsurface we have essentially a stationary boundary stretching from\neast/west near the I-96 corridor. Dew points to the north are in\nthe 60s and are in the 70s to the south. MUCAPE values are\nstratified correspondingly with 2,000 j/kg in place to the south\nand 500-1,000 to the north. The upper flow pattern will likely\nbring in convectively induced vorticity maxima from the west today\n(the IA/MN convection). Tonight models suggest a NW to SE moving\nshortwave that may bring additional storms to the area. The best\nLLJ orientation and magnitude occur early this morning through\nsunrise and again tonight coming up. Confidence in the evolution\nremains on the lower side today/tonight, but we do feel we will\nlikely see additional storms this morning, additional chances this\nafternoon, followed by more storms tonight. Wind appears to be the\nmain threat per machine learning data like NadoCast which is\nsupported by the SPC outlooks. Early afternoon soundings via the\nHRRR in Ottawa County are indicating 1,100 j/kg of DCAPE which \nshows the potential for damaging winds. \n\nPWAT values continue to be high both today and tonight with 2.0\nvalues both forecast periods. Storms have the potential to be\nmoving quickly, but rainfall rates will likely be high given deep\nsummer values of PWAT. If storms can train over certain areas or\nif motion is slower than anticipated hydro concerns will increase\n(localized short term flooding).\n\n- Thunderstorm chances may linger into Friday\n\nModels indicate the potential for some storms lingering into\nFriday morning in a northwest flow aloft just downstream of the\nplains upper ridge. SPC has a marginal threat for severe weather\nin our far southwest which makes sense given the moisture will be\npooled in this area as the front likely sags to the southwest. \n\n- Dry weather expected over the weekend\n\nThe patterns looks to try and dry out this weekend as high\npressure builds in from the north and northeast. BUFKIT moisture\noverviews this weekend are starkly drier than what we have seen\nall this week.",
